## Release Infrastructure

Behind all storefronts should be one controlled release pipeline.

Code should move through:

**Development → Staging → Internal Testing → Beta → Production**

For the Expo mobile project, EAS can manage signing credentials, production builds, and submission into Apple and Google distribution workflows.

Mobile versions and build numbers should be controlled systematically rather than manually changed by different developers. Expo's current EAS tooling can also centrally manage build-version increments.

Every production release should be traceable to a source-control commit and release record.

## Feature Flags

Do not require a new App Store release every time Mannerly wants to test a curriculum feature.

Build server-controlled feature flags.

For example:

Travel Mode enabled

Teacher Dashboard beta

New onboarding enabled

Japan Pack V2

Family Challenge system

new Passport design

new Rive scenario engine.

This allows Mannerly to release code safely and progressively enable functionality for selected accounts.

It also allows teacher, family, and consumer experiences to evolve at different speeds.

## Remote Content

Lesson content should remain primarily server-controlled.

Adding:

a dining lesson

a Japanese cultural scenario

a new language

a teacher assignment

a corrected etiquette explanation

a new animation reference

should usually not require a new native application version.

The app should download structured lesson data and appropriate media from the Mannerly backend/CDN.

Only changes requiring new application functionality should require a store release.

This keeps cultural information maintainable and allows inaccurate or outdated content to be corrected quickly.

## Crash Reporting and Operational Monitoring

Before public launch, Mannerly needs monitoring for:

application crashes

API failures

login failures

failed purchases

failed entitlement synchronization

animation failures

video failures

notification failures

account-deletion requests

parent-consent failures

class-code failures

slow lesson loading.

These should be monitored centrally across platforms while respecting the stricter privacy requirements applicable to child users.

Do not install analytics or marketing SDKs simply because they are common. Every SDK included in a mixed-age educational product should have a clear purpose and undergo privacy review.

This is especially important if Mannerly ever participates in Apple's Kids Category, where Apple imposes significant restrictions on third-party analytics, advertising, and transmission of identifiable child information.

## Push Notifications

Push notifications should use the same account-personalization model as the application.

Adults might receive:

**Today's Manner: Meeting someone new**

Parents might receive:

**Emma completed her Dining challenge**

Teachers might receive:

**8 students still have this week's lesson remaining**

Children should receive only appropriately designed learning reminders under the applicable parent and privacy controls.

Expo provides cross-platform tooling for configuring and handling push notifications on Android and iOS, although production credentials still need to be properly configured for the respective platforms.

## Support Infrastructure

Before the application appears publicly in a store, Mannerly should have a real support system.

At minimum, create:

`mannerly.com/help`

`mannerly.com/privacy`

`mannerly.com/terms`

`mannerly.com/account/delete`

and a Mannerly support email address.

The help center should cover account creation, family accounts, child profiles, teacher accounts, student codes, forgotten passwords, subscriptions, restoring purchases, changing languages, changing home region, Travel Packs, accessibility, privacy, deleting an account, and reporting incorrect cultural information.

Because cultural etiquette is part of the product itself, add:

**Report outdated or inaccurate etiquette**

to every cultural lesson.

That feedback should go into a content-review queue rather than normal technical support.

## App Review Accounts

Maintain dedicated reviewer/demo accounts for store-review teams.

Prepare an adult demo account, parent account with sample child profiles, teacher account with a sample classroom, and student account.

Provide store reviewers with clear instructions for accessing gated functionality.

Do not force an Apple or Google reviewer to guess how to reach the Teacher Dashboard, create a family, unlock premium content, or test a lesson.

A well-prepared review environment reduces unnecessary friction during submission.

## Backups and Recovery

The Mannerly backend should maintain secure database backups and documented recovery procedures.

The company needs to be able to restore user accounts, learner progress, classroom relationships, subscription entitlements, region access, and lesson content following an infrastructure failure.

Payment-provider data should never be the only record that someone legitimately owns Mannerly access.

The internal entitlement ledger should maintain enough information to reconcile store receipts with Mannerly account access.

## Recommended Distribution Order

Mannerly should not launch everywhere simultaneously.

**Phase 1:** Mannerly Web, Apple App Store, and Google Play.

**Phase 2:** Amazon Appstore if Fire Tablet adoption makes it worthwhile.

**Phase 3:** Microsoft Store PWA for school and desktop expansion.

**Phase 4:** Samsung Galaxy Store and Huawei AppGallery based on regional demand.

The codebase should be architected from the beginning so these platforms are possible, but the business should only absorb their additional operational complexity when the audience justifies it.

## The Complete User Journey

The finished Mannerly ecosystem should work like this:

A parent discovers Mannerly through Google.

They visit `mannerly.com`.

They complete a free interactive manners lesson.

They create a Family account.

They select their Home Region.

They add two children.

They install Mannerly from the App Store onto an iPad.

They sign in.

Both children's profiles immediately appear.

One child completes lessons on the iPad.

The parent later checks progress on a laptop.

A teacher sends one child a classroom code.

The parent approves the connection.

The student completes a teacher-assigned etiquette lesson.

Later, the family buys a Japan Travel Pack.

Japan appears in everyone's Mannerly Passport according to the family's entitlement rules.

The family travels.

The same lessons are available through their phones.

Nothing about the experience should make them think about Apple accounts, Google accounts, web accounts, school databases, or separate purchases.

To the customer, there is only **one Mannerly account and one Mannerly world.**

That should be the underlying goal of the distribution architecture: **many ways to enter Mannerly, one identity once the user is inside.**
